  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13959190/assets/37a9d6fca5d60e8f614730a121e1f5da23011e6c7a8ea80840a16b81e376638b.png)\n\nFind the $x$-intercepts of the circle $(x-4)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=10,$ shown above.",
  "worked_solution": "We must have $y=0$ at the $x$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =10 \\\\ (x-4)^{2}+(0+2)^{2} & =10 \\\\ (x-4)^{2}+4 & =10 \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =6 \\\\ x-4 & =\\pm \\sqrt{6} \\\\ x & =4\\pm \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts occur at the points $(4-\\sqrt{6},0)$ and $(4+\\sqrt{6},0).$",
